gbho(){return"On the beat, or off?"},
gbhn(){return"Usually the notes land right ON the beats \u2014 count 1-2-3-4 and each note lands on a number. Steady and square."},
gbhm(){return'Syncopation pushes notes OFF the beat, onto the "and" in between. The accent lands where your ear didnt expect it \u2014 thats the kick you feel in pop and jazz.'},
gbhK(){return"Two or three in a beat"},
gbhI(){return'Normally a beat splits into TWO even halves: "1-and". Two eighth notes.'},
gbhJ(){return'A triplet squeezes THREE equal notes into that same beat: "trip-o-let". It gets a little 3 above it.'},
gbgF(){return"Decorating a note"},
gbgG(){return"Ornaments are little signs that dress up a note. A trill (tr) shakes quickly between the note and the one just above it."},
gbgH(){return"A turn (a sideways S) curls AROUND the note: the note above, the note, the note below, then back. A mordent is just one quick flick up and back."},
ga52(){return"Label the Form"},
gasE(){return"Hear the sections; name the shape (ABA\u2026)"},
gb2F(){return"What is the form? (same colour = same tune)"},
gb2E(){return"Listen"},
gbfJ(){return"The shape of a piece"},
gbfI(){return"Music is built from sections. Here is a little tune \u2014 call it section A. Whenever it comes back, it is A again."},
gbfH(){return"A different tune is a new letter \u2014 section B. Tune, different tune, then the first tune again makes the form A-B-A. Lots of songs are shaped this way!"},
ga5u(){return"Which Mode?"},
gat5(){return"Major, minor or Dorian?"},
gbbf(){return"Listen! Which mode is it?"},
ga27(){return"Major"},
